# More than 16 price levels on one side of the book.
#
# The FPGA's book keeps only the best 16 levels per side. A new level that
# would push the worst one out (or one worse than all 16) means the book can
# no longer be trusted: once the better levels empty, the dropped level
# should become visible, and the FPGA no longer knows it. So the FPGA halts:
# it cancels both quotes and stops quoting, even though the market keeps
# moving.
#
# Participants: 2 and 3 provide the book, 4 adds bid depth.

latency market_data 5_000
latency order_entry 10_000
timer 1_000_000
end 20_000_000

0            2 enter 1 B 500 100.00
0            3 enter 1 S 500 100.01
0            3 enter 2 S 400 100.02

# Bid depth every other tick, 99.98 down to 99.70: with 100.00, exactly 16
# levels. The book is full but still correct, and the top doesn't change:
# no orders.
2_000_000    4 enter 1 B 100 99.98
2_001_000    4 enter 2 B 100 99.96
2_002_000    4 enter 3 B 100 99.94
2_003_000    4 enter 4 B 100 99.92
2_004_000    4 enter 5 B 100 99.90
2_005_000    4 enter 6 B 100 99.88
2_006_000    4 enter 7 B 100 99.86
2_007_000    4 enter 8 B 100 99.84
2_008_000    4 enter 9 B 100 99.82
2_009_000    4 enter 10 B 100 99.80
2_010_000    4 enter 11 B 100 99.78
2_011_000    4 enter 12 B 100 99.76
2_012_000    4 enter 13 B 100 99.74
2_013_000    4 enter 14 B 100 99.72
2_014_000    4 enter 15 B 100 99.70

# A 17th level, in the middle at 99.99, pushes 99.70 out of the book.
8_000_000    4 enter 16 B 100 99.99

# The market moves; the app no longer reacts.
10_000_000   3 cancel 1
12_000_000   2 enter 2 B 300 100.01
